  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The temperature estimates may contain uncertainties stemming from sensor calibration and the estimation of land surface emissivity.",
      "what": "This study analyzed the impact of urban expansion on land surface temperature (LST) in the Gazipur industrial zone of Bangladesh from 1995 to 2024 using satellite imagery and machine learning in Google Earth Engine. The built-up area expanded 4.4-fold from 60.46 square kilometers (3.33%) to 266.36 square kilometers (14.66%), which was associated with a mean LST increase of 1.64 °C in summer and 2.85 °C in winter. The land use classification achieved an overall accuracy between 0.92 and 0.98, with the highest temperature increases concentrated in industrialized zones like Gazipur Sadar.",
      "why": "It quantifies the direct thermal consequences of rapid, unregulated industrialization in a major South Asian manufacturing hub over a 30-year period, demonstrating a semi-automated monitoring workflow that can be scaled to other rapidly developing regions."
    }
  },
